\title{critiplotR: Visualize Risk-of-Bias Assessments with 'critiplot'}
\description{
\code{critiplotR} is an R interface to the Python package
\href{https://pypi.org/project/critiplot/}{\code{critiplot}}. It creates
publication-ready traffic-light plots and stacked bar charts for
risk-of-bias assessments in systematic reviews and meta-analyses.
}
\details{
\if{html}{\figure{logo.png}{options: width=180 alt="critiplotR logo"}}

The package supports:
\itemize{
\item the Newcastle-Ottawa Scale (NOS), via \code{\link[=plot_nos]{plot_nos()}};
\item the JBI critical appraisal checklist for case reports, via
\code{\link[=plot_jbi_case_report]{plot_jbi_case_report()}};
\item the JBI critical appraisal checklist for case series, via
\code{\link[=plot_jbi_case_series]{plot_jbi_case_series()}};
\item GRADE certainty of evidence, via \code{\link[=plot_grade]{plot_grade()}};
\item ROBIS for systematic reviews, via \code{\link[=plot_robis]{plot_robis()}};
\item the Mixed Methods Appraisal Tool (MMAT), via \code{\link[=plot_mmat]{plot_mmat()}}.
}
\subsection{Python requirement}{

A Python installation (>= 3.9) with the \code{critiplot} package is required.
On first load, \code{critiplotR} declares \code{critiplot} as a Python requirement
with \code{\link[reticulate:py_require]{reticulate::py_require()}}, so \code{reticulate} can provide it
automatically. To use an existing Python installation instead, install the
module yourself:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{reticulate::py_install("critiplot", pip = TRUE)
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Use \code{\link[reticulate:py_config]{reticulate::py_config()}} to check which Python interpreter is in use
and \code{\link[reticulate:py_module_available]{reticulate::py_module_available()}} to check that \code{critiplot} is
available.
}

\subsection{Input data}{

Every plotting function accepts a data frame or a path to a CSV (\code{.csv}) or
Excel (\code{.xls}/\code{.xlsx}) file. The required columns are those expected by the
corresponding \code{critiplot} function. The package README lists the column
names and accepted values for each plot type. Bundled example files are
available through \code{\link[=system.file]{system.file()}}, for example:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode r">}}\preformatted{system.file("extdata", "sample_nos.csv", package = "critiplotR")
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

